he gist of  the book is just the story of me growing  up with my mom and it's a story of  coming-of-age you know it's a story of  being an outsider not knowing where you  fit in the world not knowing how the  world relates to you and I think a lot  of kids  related to that and so that's been the  driving force for why the book has  remained on the New York Times  bestsellers list I've been really  grateful the the book does segue back to  that bit we played at the top where  people were talking about camping and  you're like look that was my life I mean  there are moments in the book where if I  recall correctly you guys have so little  money you're eating wild spinach just  picked off the ground  it's an unusual story in addition to  being Universal in the sense that it is  coming of age as you as you repackage it  leave some things in take other things  out for young readers in this new  edition what are you hoping that they'll  take away from it well the biggest thing  I hope they take away is what you know I  took forever to learn and we all take  forever to learn in life especially as  kids is is that you know in life I don't  think we should be striving to fit in we  should be striving to find ourselves we  should be striving to accept ourselves  and you know I know a lot of kids read  my book and they were like oh wow I  can't believe you know you grew up in  South Africa but you have the same  stresses as me you were you you know you  were worried about getting someone to go  to the prom with you were worried about  you know being liked in school you were  worried about where you fit in your  family was dysfunctional and you you had  issues with that and those issues came  into your life and and so for me  whenever children read the book and  whenever I read passages to kids or  whenever I join in to like little book  clubs the the biggest thing that strikes  me is how similar we are all over the  globe you know we we all have different  lives and we all live in different  countries but when it boils down to it  as human beings there are fundamental  experiences that we're all going through  on very different levels and it's  actually unifying to see for me that  that no matter where you are in the  world no matter what continent you want  you can be experiencing the exact same  thing and I think you can find the same  answers in how to overcome those  obstacles  Trevor Maier used to say that all the  time that we are more alike than we are  different in your book certainly  highlights that the the original version  was 84,000 words for young adults it's  72,000 words you say no profanity no  overt sexual material but in the book  you include  apartheid and how it differs from Jim  Crow laws in the United States I have  questions about that but I won't  why it's important you think to have  those kind of discussions with kids well  I think it's important you know when I  was when I was young I I had a nap and I  had an amazing history teacher in school  who really emphasized the need for us to  learn history and I never understood why  and she always used to say juries used  to say the reason we learn history is so  we can try our best not to repeat the  follies of the past and I think a lot of  the time history and learning of history  has become a divisive thing in many  places in the world because people feel  like we're trying to relive what  happened but I think if anything it  gives us context not just as kids but as  adults in learning where we've come from  how we've come to be here and and I  think you know if you think of history  on a simple level if you remove humans  from it it just helps us understand why  we are where we are today you know so so  if you're going to build a house you get  somebody to come out and survey the land  and they're basically telling you the  history of the land before you can build  the house so you can figure out what  foundation to put on that land and I  think history serves the same purpose  for us without us knowing what the  foundation of the land is we can't  figure out how to move forward as a  society we don't know why anybody is  where they are we don't understand what  structural elements of society have been  in place that have hurt people or help  certain people and so I think that's the  importance with histories just  understanding this is how we got to  where we are today and then this is how  we can move away from what we did  incorrectly in the past and I think  that's the beauty of storytelling that's  the beauty of history and I think people  fights about it way way more than they  should because they put themselves in  the history and then they start to fight  about something that has already  happened yeah we need people to map  those foundations for us and we also  need people to take the particular and  to make it Universal and in that latter  category you're one of the best there is  so Trevor Noah thank you very much for  joining us the paperback edition of  think it's Trevor Noah born a crime is  on sale now you can see the daily show  by the way distancing the daily chef  distancing show weeknights on Comedy  Central
 

